67b5c97bb17f5b011830031a,Ebubechukwu Okoro,+2348138913348,eokoroeziaha@gmail.com,Nigeria,"Graduating as a Soil Scientist in my home country Nigeria, avails you almost no opportunity to put your knowledge to work, causing most graduates in my field to change career paths. Despite the daunting challenges, I have not been dissuaded from driving for positive agricultural and environmental change in my locality. \n\nThe current dilapidation of the environment in Nigeria is alarming, the poor energy utilization and non-adoption of sustainable development practices, are so concerning that they incentivized me to seek more relevant knowledge to enable me address these issues in Nigeria. \n\nMy quest for positive environmental and sustainability inclusion began during my undergraduate studies; in my second year, I led my class on a community service activity titled “Our Soil, Our Lives”, where we cleaned the streets of Choba, Rivers State, Nigeria, replaced roadside dumping grounds with 30litres waste bins, cleared the drainage and planted ornamental crops such as “Queen of Night” and “Flame of the Woods” to prevent further refuse dumping and better protect the environment. There was also public sensitization on the benefits of environmental protection and recommended small-scale farming as an alternative to land abuse or land fallowing, effectively producing food that can be consumed by the households in the community and also sold for profit. This community service was spurred by the Shell Petroleum Development Company (SPDC) scholarship award I received as financial assistance to cover tuition and upkeep; this service was me giving back to the community.\n\nHaving financial challenges while in school presents you with a lot of disadvantages, multiplies the pressure and increases the chances of failure as a student, but thankfully I was able to overcome by the help of the scholarship and also by starting up a food supply business, where I acted as the middleman between the consumers and the producers. This was at a time when online shopping was not so popular in Nigeria, so I was able to take orders from households, made the purchases on their behalf and delivered at their doorsteps, I earned commissions for every purchase from the producers/suppliers and the service charges paid by the households/consumers.\n\nWhile in the university, I participated in the Faculty of Agriculture Students’ Assembly, where I was a member of Parliament out of the three (3) representing my class. This was a launch pad for me to take up other leadership roles within the academic community in my school, which included being a pioneer member of AGRIPRENEUR – a student-based organization that trained students on agribusiness, entrepreneurship and how to monetize the skills we were obtaining from the university at the time, I also went ahead to become the Assistant General Secretary of the organization.\n\nUpon graduation, I completed a one-year voluntary national service. During this time, I educated secondary school children on Agricultural Science, Biology and Earth Protection. I also led a team of other volunteers to the Karu Local Government Area (LGA) in Nasarawa State, Nigeria, where we set up a community farmer’s association for the rural farming communities in the LGA, exposed them to crops and soil protection techniques, proper fertilizer application and aided them in accessing government and non-government agricultural loans for their farms. In addition to these, during my voluntary national service, I was also part of a group called The Gender Vanguard, which was established to address the issues of gender-based violence and the focus during my time with them, was to identify and help young boys who were victims of abuse but overlooked due to their gender and we were able to help over 10 young boys from the North-Central region of Nigeria.\n\nMy volunteer work extended beyond my service days as I signed up with the Ileka Foundation Nigeria, to participate in outreaches and campaigns for girl-child health awareness, help in organizing outreaches, purchase project materials and visit internally displaced persons camps for health sensitization and sustainability.\n\nAt my current workplace, I work as a Project Assistant, where I work with the project manager to oversee production and processing on the two farms owned and managed by the company. I have 14 staff covering 5 departments working under me and I ensure that the company’s goals are not only met to precision but also exceeded where possible. I have also been involved in consulting for our business partners on the sustainability of our practices and the profitability of each action we have taken on the farm to achieve these goals. My success at my job, got me recommended to be sent to Port Novo, Benin Republic for further training and internship to take on managerial roles that combine both the technical and administrative sectors of our establishment.\n\nI love new challenges and I am always eager to contribute beneficially to systems where I find myself and I will not stop until I have made the desired impact on the Agricultural sphere in Nigeria.",,https://dev-talent-form-test.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/uploads/1739966839538_Copy of Ebubechukwu OKORO CV.pdf,67b35613797571ab1a647f1c,Business Development & Marketing Manager,2025-02-19T12:07:23.779Z,2025-02-19T12:07:23.779Z,,,,,,,,,
